    (v_topic_id, 'A 72-year-old male named James with end-stage metastatic lung cancer is actively dying in the hospice unit. He has severe dyspnea, using accessory muscles of respiration. Pulse oximetry shows oxygen saturation of 93% on room air. What is the most appropriate first-line pharmacological intervention to relieve his dyspnea?', '["Administer low-dose immediate-release morphine (oral or intravenous), as opioids are the first-line pharmacotherapy for terminal dyspnea by reducing the perception of air hunger and blunting the respiratory drive", "Initiate high-flow nasal cannula oxygen at 40 L/min to raise his oxygen saturation to 100%", "Prescribe oral acetazolamide to induce a metabolic acidosis and stimulate deep hyperventilation", "Administer intravenous albuterol and start an epinephrine nebulizer to dilate the pulmonary vasculature", "Initiate a continuous intravenous infusion of midazolam at a high dose to induce general anesthesia"]'::jsonb, 0, 'For the management of **dyspnea (air hunger) at the end of life**, **low-dose opioids (predominantly morphine)** are the **first-line pharmacological treatment of choice**. Opioids work by: (1) Reducing the central perception of dyspnea, (2) Blunting the ventilatory response to hypoxia and hypercapnia, and (3) Reducing anxiety and pulmonary congestion (vasodilation). **Oxygen is only indicated if the patient is hypoxic** (O2 saturation < 90-92%); studies show oxygen is no more effective than room air for non-hypoxic dyspnea. Non-pharmacological measures include a cool fan blowing air across the face. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 82; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 66-year-old male named Albert is in the active phase of dying. He is comatose. The family is extremely distressed by the onset of loud, wet, gurgling respirations (''death rattle''). What is the most appropriate clinical management for this symptom?', '["Perform deep endotracheal suctioning every 15 minutes to manually clear all accumulated bronchial secretions", "Administer intravenous furosemide 80 mg every 2 hours to dry the lungs, and restrict all oral hygiene measures", "Reposition the patient (turn them on their side or elevate the head of the bed), reassure the family that the patient is not distresses or choking, and administer an anticholinergic agent (e.g., hyoscyamine) to dry secretions; avoid deep suctioning", "Initiate a rapid intravenous infusion of normal saline to dilute the secretions and make them easier for the comatose patient to expectorate", "Insert a laryngeal mask airway (LMA) and initiate positive pressure ventilation to blow the secretions back into the alveoli"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The **terminal ''death rattle''** is caused by the movement of saliva or secretions in the hypopharynx of a dying patient who has lost the swallowing and cough reflexes. It is highly distressing to the family but does not cause distress to the comatose patient. Management: (1) **Family reassurance** (explain that the patient is not choking). (2) **Repositioning** (lateral recumbent position or semi-Fowler''s position to allow gravity drainage). (3) **Anticholinergic drugs** (e.g., **glycopyrrolate**, **scopolamine patch**, **hyoscyamine**, or sublingual atropine drops) to reduce further secretion production. **Deep suctioning should be avoided** as it is highly invasive, causes discomfort/agitation, and stimulates further secretion production via mucosal trauma. (Ref: UWorld Step 2 CK; Hazzard''s Ch. 82)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 74-year-old female named Phyllis is in the active phase of dying. She is comatose. The family is extremely distressed by the onset of loud, wet, gurgling respirations (''death rattle''). What is the most appropriate clinical management for this symptom?', '["Reposition the patient (turn them on their side or elevate the head of the bed), reassure the family that the patient is not distresses or choking, and administer an anticholinergic agent (e.g., glycopyrrolate) to dry secretions; avoid deep suctioning", "Administer intravenous furosemide 80 mg every 2 hours to dry the lungs, and restrict all oral hygiene measures", "Initiate a rapid intravenous infusion of normal saline to dilute the secretions and make them easier for the comatose patient to expectorate", "Insert a laryngeal mask airway (LMA) and initiate positive pressure ventilation to blow the secretions back into the alveoli", "Perform deep endotracheal suctioning every 15 minutes to manually clear all accumulated bronchial secretions"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The **terminal ''death rattle''** is caused by the movement of saliva or secretions in the hypopharynx of a dying patient who has lost the swallowing and cough reflexes. It is highly distressing to the family but does not cause distress to the comatose patient. Management: (1) **Family reassurance** (explain that the patient is not choking). (2) **Repositioning** (lateral recumbent position or semi-Fowler''s position to allow gravity drainage). (3) **Anticholinergic drugs** (e.g., **glycopyrrolate**, **scopolamine patch**, **hyoscyamine**, or sublingual atropine drops) to reduce further secretion production. **Deep suctioning should be avoided** as it is highly invasive, causes discomfort/agitation, and stimulates further secretion production via mucosal trauma. (Ref: UWorld Step 2 CK; Hazzard''s Ch. 82)'),
