    (v_topic_id, 'A 88-year-old male named James is in the active phase of dying. He is comatose. The family is extremely distressed by the onset of loud, wet, gurgling respirations (''death rattle''). What is the most appropriate clinical management for this symptom?', '["Initiate a rapid intravenous infusion of normal saline to dilute the secretions and make them easier for the comatose patient to expectorate", "Reposition the patient (turn them on their side or elevate the head of the bed), reassure the family that the patient is not distresses or choking, and administer an anticholinergic agent (e.g., scopolamine transdermal patch) to dry secretions; avoid deep suctioning", "Insert a laryngeal mask airway (LMA) and initiate positive pressure ventilation to blow the secretions back into the alveoli", "Perform deep endotracheal suctioning every 15 minutes to manually clear all accumulated bronchial secretions", "Administer intravenous furosemide 80 mg every 2 hours to dry the lungs, and restrict all oral hygiene measures"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The **terminal ''death rattle''** is caused by the movement of saliva or secretions in the hypopharynx of a dying patient who has lost the swallowing and cough reflexes. It is highly distressing to the family but does not cause distress to the comatose patient. Management: (1) **Family reassurance** (explain that the patient is not choking). (2) **Repositioning** (lateral recumbent position or semi-Fowler''s position to allow gravity drainage). (3) **Anticholinergic drugs** (e.g., **glycopyrrolate**, **scopolamine patch**, **hyoscyamine**, or sublingual atropine drops) to reduce further secretion production. **Deep suctioning should be avoided** as it is highly invasive, causes discomfort/agitation, and stimulates further secretion production via mucosal trauma. (Ref: UWorld Step 2 CK; Hazzard''s Ch. 82)'),
