    (v_topic_id, 'A 82-year-old female named Rose is in the final days of life in an inpatient hospice unit. She develops acute hyperactive delirium, characterized by severe agitation, plucking at the air, and distressing visual hallucinations. Non-pharmacological interventions are maximized. Which of the following is the most appropriate first-line pharmacological treatment?', '["Oral methylphenidate to stimulate the prefrontal cortex and restore normal sleep-wake cycles", "Intravenous physostigmine to reverse the age-related cholinergic excess in the central nervous system", "Oral diphenhydramine at high doses to sedate the patient and prevent falls", "Continuous infusion of lorazepam monotherapy, as benzodiazepines are the preferred agents to resolve geriatric delirium", "Low-dose haloperidol (or atypical antipsychotics like olanzapine or quetiapine), as antipsychotics are the first-line pharmacotherapy for distressing hyperactive terminal delirium"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Terminal delirium** affects up to 88% of patients in their final days. Hyperactive delirium (agitation, hallucinations, cognitive distress) should be managed pharmacologically if it causes distress to the patient or safety risks. **Low-dose haloperidol** (or atypical antipsychotics like olanzapine) is the **first-line drug of choice** because it targets dopamine receptors without causing excessive respiratory depression. **Benzodiazepines (e.g., lorazepam) should be avoided as monotherapy** because they frequently cause **paradoxical agitation** and worsen delirium in older adults, though they can be added as adjuncts for severe, refractory agitation or when terminal sedation is indicated. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 82; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
