    (v_topic_id, 'A 82-year-old female named Ruth has decided to withdraw from hemodialysis and is receiving palliative care. She develops severe, persistent uremic nausea and vomiting. Which of the following antiemetics is most appropriate as first-line therapy, and what is its primary site of action?', '["Metoclopramide; which acts as a pure serotonin antagonist in the cerebral cortex", "Ondansetron; which acts as an anticholinergic agent in the vestibular nuclei", "Haloperidol; which acts as a potent dopamine (D2) receptor antagonist in the chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ) of the brainstem, which is stimulated by uremic toxins", "Aprepitant; which blocks histamine receptors in the stomach wall", "Scopolamine; which acts as a prokinetic agent in the gastroduodenal junction"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Nausea and vomiting at the end of life must be treated based on the underlying etiology. In **uremia (end-stage renal failure)**, uremic toxins accumulate and stimulate the **chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ)** in the area postrema of the medulla. The CTZ is rich in dopamine (D2) and serotonin (5-HT3) receptors. **Haloperidol** is a highly effective, first-line antiemetic for **chemical/toxin-induced nausea** due to its potent central D2-receptor antagonism. Metoclopramide (prokinetic) is preferred for gastroparesis. Scopolamine is preferred for vestibular nausea. (Ref: Oxford Handbook of Palliative Care; Hazzard''s Ch. 82)'),
