    (v_topic_id, 'A 76-year-old male named James has decided to withdraw from hemodialysis and is receiving palliative care. He develops severe, persistent uremic nausea and vomiting. Which of the following antiemetics is most appropriate as first-line therapy, and what is its primary site of action?', '["Scopolamine; which acts as a prokinetic agent in the gastroduodenal junction", "Ondansetron; which acts as an anticholinergic agent in the vestibular nuclei", "Haloperidol; which acts as a potent dopamine (D2) receptor antagonist in the chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ) of the brainstem, which is stimulated by uremic toxins", "Metoclopramide; which acts as a pure serotonin antagonist in the cerebral cortex", "Aprepitant; which blocks histamine receptors in the stomach wall"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Nausea and vomiting at the end of life must be treated based on the underlying etiology. In **uremia (end-stage renal failure)**, uremic toxins accumulate and stimulate the **chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ)** in the area postrema of the medulla. The CTZ is rich in dopamine (D2) and serotonin (5-HT3) receptors. **Haloperidol** is a highly effective, first-line antiemetic for **chemical/toxin-induced nausea** due to its potent central D2-receptor antagonism. Metoclopramide (prokinetic) is preferred for gastroparesis. Scopolamine is preferred for vestibular nausea. (Ref: Oxford Handbook of Palliative Care; Hazzard''s Ch. 82)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 70-year-old male named Leonard with terminal peritoneal carcinomatosis has an inoperable malignant bowel obstruction. He has severe colic (cramping abdominal pain) and nausea. Which of the following antiemetics/prokinetics should be **avoided** in this patient, and why?', '["Avoid dexamethasone; because it stimulates intestinal secretions and worsens abdominal distension", "Avoid metoclopramide; because as a prokinetic agent, it stimulates gastric and intestinal motility against a fixed, complete obstruction, which can cause severe colic, abdominal pain, and bowel perforation", "Avoid octreotide; because it accelerates gastric emptying and increases stool volume", "Avoid ondansetron; because it cause immediate cardiovascular collapse in patients with cancer", "Avoid glycopyrrolate; because it causes severe diarrhea by hyperactivating the parasympathetic nervous system"]'::jsonb, 1, 'In patients with a **complete, inoperable malignant bowel obstruction**, prokinetic agents such as **metoclopramide must be avoided**. Metoclopramide increases upper gastrointestinal motility and contracts the stomach and duodenum. Stimulating peristalsis against a closed, mechanical obstruction leads to **severe cramping pain (colic)** and increases the risk of **gastrointestinal perforation**. Instead, management should focus on antisecretory agents (octreotide), anti-inflammatory steroids (dexamethasone), and anticholinergic antispasmodics (glycopyrrolate) to reduce secretions and colic. (Ref: Oxford Handbook of Palliative Care; Hazzard''s Ch. 82; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
